  ('408266d7-4bba-5ce5-b099-57a9920b7ea1', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'graphing-linear-functions'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The graph of $f(x) = -\frac{3}{4}x + b$ passes through $(8, -1)$. Where does the graph cross the $x$-axis?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$x = \\frac{20}{3}$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$x = 5$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"gave-y-intercept"},{"text":"$x = -\\frac{28}{3}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"sign-error-solving-for-b"},{"text":"$x = \\frac{15}{4}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"multiplied-instead-of-divided"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$x = \frac{20}{3}$$q$, $q$First find $b$: $-1 = -\frac{3}{4}(8) + b = -6 + b$, so $b = 5$. Then set $f(x) = 0$: $-\frac{3}{4}x + 5 = 0$, so $x = 5 \div \frac{3}{4} = \frac{20}{3}$. The value $5$ is the $y$-intercept, where the graph crosses the $y$-axis.$q$, 'draft', null)
